        "type": "thinking",
        "thinking": "I need to decide between relative imports and the package-style import since the file lives in samples — I'll go with relative paths as the GUIDE specifies, despite the system prompt referencing an \"agentic-3js-builder\" module elsewhere.\n\nNow I'm planning the actual build: creating joints and a catmull-rom chain for the spine, then sweeping it with a radius function and n-gon cross-section. Writing paint shader for skin texture with noise-based tone mixing and cellular wrinkle borders...\n\nI'm adding directional lighting so the top surfaces read warm and lit while the belly stays darker and cooler, plus a dusty reddish tint on upward-facing areas. Then I'm figuring out how to shade the ear separately, approximating distance from the rim to lighten the skin near the edges.\n\nFor the ear's coloring, I'm computing a radial rim factor to darken the edges with grey-brown tones and add pinkish mottling in the middle, then layering thin veins radiating outward from the attachment point using angle-based noise. Now I'm reconsidering the tusk's grain pattern, since a simple y-axis stripe doesn't match its curved shape.\n\nFor the tusk I'm instead painting fine streaks along the tube using the path parameter and angle, darkening the root with a brownish stain, keeping the tip pale with a worn cap look, and applying the paint across the whole surface rather than per-sweep band. Nails just get flat colors, and for the foot I'm shaping a dark-skinned sole cylinder with a toe pad on top.\n\nNow I'm building the eye texture as an SVG: a wrinkled skin ring forms the lid, with layered ellipses for the eye socket, iris in amber brown, a dark pupil, and a white highlight for shine.\n\nStill writing SVG textures...\n\nI'm also adding a mud splash card with irregular brown blobs scattered on the lower legs for extra character, bringing the texture set to eye, crease, bristle, and mud.\n\nI'm sketching out the part count target of around 200+ and setting up the builder config with a name, detail level, and paint atlas size, reasoning through texel density to decide 1024 is sufficient resolution for wrinkle detail without being too heavy.\n\nSetting up vector type helper...\n\nI'm checking that flat shading still plays nicely with painted UVs for mixed part types, then sketching a mirroring helper using a sides array of 1 and -1 to handle left/right naming conventions.\n\nNow I'm defining leg chains, working out joint positions for each leg using scaled x-offsets and settling on naming conventions like shoulder/elbow/wrist/foot or hip/knee/ankle/foot for the front and back limbs.\n\nI'll define a leg radius function that tapers in two segments, narrowing from the thigh down to the ankle, with flat caps at the foot since that gets hidden by the foot cylinder anyway. I'll use a 9-sided ngon for the leg sweep cross-section, and consider adding a slight widening near the ankle for a \"sock\" effect.\n\nNow I'm adding wrinkle rings at the elbow and knee joints using flattened tori positioned along the leg chain, with a helper function that places each ring using the chain point's position and tangent direction, plus the nearest bone for rigging.\n\n",
